## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design automated, cross-account containment with tenant isolation, key lifecycle control, and immutable logs. Demonstrates threat modeling, policy boundaries, and incident runbook integration.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation across multiple AWS accounts
- Tenant-scoped KMS keys with rotation and key policy constraints
- Anomaly detection via CloudTrail Insights and GuardDuty
- Automated containment using EventBridge + Step Functions
- Immutable audit trails in CloudTrail and S3

## Code Example
```json
// Example KMS Key Policy (tenant-scoped)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "tenant-key-policy",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/tenant-access"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"aws:SourceAccount": "TENANT_ACCOUNT"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end before production?
- What metrics signal health of the containment and prevent false positives?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account trust, least privilege S3/KMS usage, no Internet constraint, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account role trust
- S3 bucket policy with path-level scoping
- KMS policy for decrypt/encrypt
- No Internet access
- Immutable auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy excerpt for DataAccessRole
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ProcessorRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// Bucket policy excerpt (data-inbound)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound/data-inbound/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-out/*"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// KMS key policy snippet (CMK in Security account)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey*","kms:Encrypt"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for privilege creep?
- How would you rotate the CMK without downtime?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, secure cross-account access with ephemeral credentials, tenant isolation, and proactive breach testing in a real-world multi-account data pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Roles Anywhere + OIDC for short-lived, tenant-scoped credentials
- Lake Formation per-tenant access controls
- Cross-account trust with least privilege
- CMK rotation and key policy governance
- Automated breach simulations, revocation, comprehensive auditing

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kinesis:DescribeStream",
        "kinesis:GetRecords",
        "kinesis:GetShardIterator"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:account-a:region:stream/tenant-*-stream",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/TenantID": "${aws:username}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement tenant isolation with least privilege, plus rapid containment using AWS-native controls. It also covers S3 prefix scoping, per-tenant KMS keys, CloudTrail data events, and automated revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Least privilege IAM for Lambda and prefix-scoped S3 access
- S3 bucket policies with bucket-level and object-prefix constraints
- Per-tenant KMS keys and rotation
- CloudTrail Data Events for S3 access
- EventBridge-based containment workflow (60s)

## Code Example
```javascript
// IAM policy snippet for Lambda role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-id-${aws:PrincipalTag tenantId}/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-id-*"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle role cred leakage or bucket misconfiguration?
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end?
